// single, unified Next.js application, evaluating whether to split
// into micro frontends.

// EVIDENCE GATHERING (directly mirroring Module 27 lesson 6's own
// evidence-gathering structure):

// Team structure: the PRODUCT CATALOG/browsing experience is owned
// by a 12-person team with their own, independent release schedule,
// shipping new catalog features multiple times per WEEK. The
// CHECKOUT flow is owned by a SEPARATE, 4-person team that
// deliberately releases far less frequently, with significantly
// more rigorous, slower review (genuinely high-stakes, payment-
// related code) - these two teams' actual, real release cadences
// have become a genuine, observed source of conflict and delay
// when forced through ONE single, shared, monolithic deployment pipeline

// CONCLUSION: this SPECIFIC evidence (genuinely, dramatically
// different team release cadences, causing genuine, observed
// coordination friction) justifies extracting the PRODUCT CATALOG
// into its own, independently-deployable micro frontend specifically
// - NOT a wholesale split of EVERY section into separate micro
// frontends, directly matching Module 27 lesson 6's own deliberate,
// PARTIAL extraction pattern, simply applied to frontend deployment
// cadence rather than backend scaling needs

